rsetup-controller — notes for Debian users
==========================================

What this package provides
--------------------------

  /usr/bin/rsetup-controller            loopback Web control centre (controller)
  /usr/bin/rsetup-controller-migrate    explicit, operator-only schema migration
  /usr/bin/rsetup-admin                   local root emergency admin recovery
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/rsetup-controller.service
  /usr/share/doc/rsetup-controller/rsetup-controller.default  configuration example, 0644, no secrets

The controller talks to MySQL/TiDB, serves the embedded web UI, and listens on
127.0.0.1:8080 unless told otherwise. It does not terminate TLS.

Packaging is delivered here; running a migration and configuring a real target
are operator actions. Nothing in this package has connected to a real database,
migrated one, or enabled the service: no step below should be read as already
done, and the package deliberately does not do any of them on your behalf.

The package installs the unit but does not enable it, does not start it, and does
not restart or stop it on upgrade (dh_installsystemd --no-enable --no-start
--no-stop-on-upgrade): the generated maintainer scripts carry no start, restart
or stop call on the install and upgrade paths (neither `deb-systemd-invoke
start`/`restart`/`stop` nor the `_dh_action` variant), and beyond debhelper's
unit state bookkeeping the only thing installing does is create the service
account. The configuration example ships as documentation at
/usr/share/doc/rsetup-controller/rsetup-controller.default only — the package
installs no file under /etc — and until you create the configuration under its
real name (section 1, one `install -m0600` command), the unit's
ConditionPathExists=/etc/default/rsetup-controller keeps the service inactive:
a start request is skipped rather than failing loudly, and `systemctl status`
shows `inactive (dead)` with the failed `ConditionPathExists` in its output.

One lifecycle is deliberately outside that promise: *removing* the package
(`dpkg --remove` / `--purge`) may stop a running controller. That branch is
debhelper's own bookkeeping, not this package's choice — from debhelper 13.25
the generated prerm always stops the service on remove (official changelog,
Debian bug #1079181) — and it is why upgrades, unlike removals, never touch the
running process.

1. Configure the service
------------------------

  sudo install -m0600 -o root -g root \
    /usr/share/doc/rsetup-controller/rsetup-controller.default \
    /etc/default/rsetup-controller
  sudoedit /etc/default/rsetup-controller

Create the file with its final ownership and mode in one step, then edit it in
place: sudoedit keeps the mode of the file it opens, so the file never exists at
0644 while it already carries a DSN. Do NOT `cp` it first and tighten the mode
afterwards — between the copy and the chmod the future EnvironmentFile is world
readable, and an operator who fills the database URL before that chmod exposes it.

Uncomment and fill in the four required variables (CONTROLLER_DATABASE_URL,
CONTROLLER_ALLOWED_HOSTS, CONTROLLER_ALLOWED_ORIGIN,
CONTROLLER_MANAGEMENT_CURSOR_SECRET_HEX). The shipped documentation example is
0644 and carries no secrets; the package deliberately does not install any file
into /etc, so nothing at /etc/default/rsetup-controller exists until you create
it. Verify before and after editing:

  ls -l /etc/default/rsetup-controller     # -rw------- root root (0600)

Keep the file out of any backup or ticket that is not secret-handling.

2. Back up the target database, then set the migration gates
------------------------------------------------------------

Take a real, restorable backup of the database the service points at, and note
the artefact you produced (file, checksum, backup tool reference). The four
gates below are how that attestation reaches the migration tool; it never
guesses on your behalf:

  CONTROLLER_DATABASE_URL                 the same variable the service uses
  CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE  must be exactly 1
  CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_EXPECTED_DATABASE  must equal the real connected database name
  CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_BACKUP_REF         a non-blank reference to the backup you took
  CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_ACK                exactly: isolated-exclusive-backed-up-disposable

The acknowledgement states "target isolated and exclusively held, real backup
exists". For a production database it does not mean the data may be discarded.
Pass them in a throwaway environment rather than leaving them in the unit's
EnvironmentFile, for example:

  sudo env \
    CONTROLLER_DATABASE_URL='...' \
    CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=1 \
    CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_EXPECTED_DATABASE='<real database name>' \
    CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_BACKUP_REF='/srv/backups/rsc-2026-01-01.sha256' \
    CONTROLLER_MIGRATION_ACK='isolated-exclusive-backed-up-disposable' \
    rsetup-controller-migrate --apply

The tool requires no root: write access comes from the database account in the
URL, and the safety comes from the gates plus the privileges of that account.
It never reads /dev/tty and prompts for nothing.

3. Check, then migrate
----------------------

  sudo rsetup-controller-migrate --check
  # prints exactly one line: schema_version=<number> or schema_version=missing

  sudo rsetup-controller-migrate --apply

--check is read-only, needs only CONTROLLER_DATABASE_URL, and prints a version
number. That is a version probe, not the full shape check: it does not tell you
whether every table, column and foreign key is present and correct.

--apply is v4-only, and its decision is taken from the version row before any DDL
runs, with exactly three outcomes:

  * no version row and zero existing tables — the complete v4 baseline is
    created. The fresh-create entry refuses a database that already carries any
    table, so it never builds on top of an unknown starting point;
  * version 4 — a read-only verification pass, zero DDL and zero writes, which is
    why re-running it is a repeatable no-op;
  * any other version (v1, v2, v3, 0, a negative number, v5 or later) — refused.
    There is no upgrade path, no legacy fixture mode and no in-place repair of a
    partially created schema; the tool never drops a database either.

The fresh create is a sequence of separate DDL statements, not one transaction. If
a statement fails, or a created object disagrees with the schema metadata, the tool
stops where it is, reports the failure and leaves behind the tables it had already
made. It does not roll them back, and it does not continue that build on a later
run: the next --apply is refused at the zero-tables gate above. It never
auto-repairs and never reports a success it did not verify. Getting out of that
state is a manual, separately authorised decision by you, taken against the backup
from step 2 — clearing the objects of that isolated database is not something this
tool will do on your behalf.

All failures print fixed, de-identified messages: no DSN, database name, backup
path or driver text. And none of the above happens by itself: this command is the
only thing that runs DDL, and starting the service performs no schema DDL work.
First-start administrator bootstrap still writes application data as described in
section 5; the read-only schema check must pass before that bootstrap runs.

4. Start the service
--------------------

  sudo systemctl start rsetup-controller
  systemctl status rsetup-controller
  journalctl -u rsetup-controller -n 50

Start it manually on purpose. To have it start at boot once everything is in
place: sudo systemctl enable --now rsetup-controller.

Installing or upgrading the package never starts, restarts or stops this
service: if it was running it keeps running on the old process until you
restart it yourself (sudo systemctl restart rsetup-controller), and if it was
stopped it stays stopped. That is deliberate — a restart means reconnecting to
a real database with a configuration the upgrade may not match. Only removing
the package stops it (see the note at the top of this file).

5. First boot: read the one-time administrator credential
--------------------------------------------------------

On the first start against a freshly migrated schema the service creates the
built-in administrator and writes the one-time credential to

  /var/lib/rsetup-controller/bootstrap-secret.log     # mode 0600, owned by the unit

Read it immediately and delete the file:

  sudo cat /var/lib/rsetup-controller/bootstrap-secret.log
  sudo rm -f /var/lib/rsetup-controller/bootstrap-secret.log

Log in at http://127.0.0.1:8080 with that credential and change the password
right away. The file is created with O_EXCL: if it already exists the service
refuses to overwrite it, so a leftover file means an earlier boot, not this one.

6. Losing the administrator account
-----------------------------------

  sudo rsetup-admin

Run this directly on the controller host, as root, from an interactive
terminal: it requires real and effective UID 0, interactive stdin/stdout/
stderr and a writable /dev/tty, and prints the temporary password only on
/dev/tty, exactly once, after the reset transaction committed. It accepts no
username or password arguments.

7. Exposure
-----------

The default bind address is loopback only, and the HTTP surface assumes a
controlled management network: no TLS termination, no untrusted-network
hardening, no multi-tenant isolation. If it must be reachable beyond this host,
put it behind a reverse proxy with TLS and a trusted network, then keep
CONTROLLER_ALLOWED_HOSTS and CONTROLLER_ALLOWED_ORIGIN aligned with what the
proxy forwards — a mismatch is rejected by the service, not silently allowed.

Unit hardening you should know about
------------------------------------

The unit runs as the static system account rsetup-controller (User= and
Group=, plus StateDirectory=rsetup-controller with StateDirectoryMode=0700).
The package creates that account and its private group on install with
`adduser --system --group --home /var/lib/rsetup-controller --no-create-home
--disabled-login`, idempotently.
The package never removes that account: there is no `deluser` step anywhere in its
maintainer scripts (the only generated postrm content is debhelper's systemd state
cleanup for the unit file), so after
`dpkg --remove` and even after `dpkg --purge` the account, its private group and
its uid/gid stay in the system databases, and so does the persistent state under
/var/lib/rsetup-controller (kept on purpose for forensics and data
preservation — the purge does not delete a database, and it does not delete what
the service wrote next to it). A later reinstall reuses that same account, which
is what keeps the state directory's owner stable. To clean the identity up
yourself once you are sure you will not reinstall:

  sudo deluser --system rsetup-controller      # removes the account, then the group
  sudo rm -rf /var/lib/rsetup-controller       # only once the data is really unwanted

The uid/gid is allocated once and then stays put: /var/lib/rsetup-controller has
one stable owner (mode 0700, from StateDirectoryMode=0700,
rsetup-controller:rsetup-controller) across restarts and upgrades, so backup
and rsync policies, ACLs and "who wrote this file" forensics keep working.
That stability is exactly why the unit does not use DynamicUser=yes — a dynamic
user gets a fresh temporary uid on every start and systemd re-chowns the state
directory each time, so ownership of persistent state drifts on every restart.
Do not pre-create /var/lib/rsetup-controller with a different owner; systemd
creates and owns it. If you take over an installation that used a dynamic user,
chown the existing state once:
`sudo chown -R rsetup-controller:rsetup-controller /var/lib/rsetup-controller`.
ProtectSystem=strict makes /usr and /etc read-only for the service, so
/etc/default/rsetup-controller is its only configuration input,
RestrictAddressFamilies limits it to IPv4/IPv6/Unix sockets, and UMask=0077 makes
every file the process creates owner-readable-only by default (the one-time
bootstrap credential is created 0600 explicitly and is unaffected by the mask).
